<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Ghana</h1><p class="st-meta">1957 CE – present · capital Accra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">West African republic, in 1957 the first Sub-Saharan African colony to gain sovereignty, with multi-party elections since 1992 after coups and military rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 6 March 1957 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ashanti-colony">Ashanti Colony</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-togoland">British Togoland</a> were united as an independent dominion within the Commonwealth under the name Ghana. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party">Convention People's Party</a> had campaigned for self-government, became prime minister, and he declared a republic on 1 July 1960.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nkrumah repressed political opposition, and a constitutional amendment of 1964 made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> with him as president for life. He was overthrown on 24 February 1966 in a coup, and military and civilian governments alternated until Jerry John Rawlings took power in 1981. A new constitution restored multi-party politics in 1992, and Rawlings was elected that year and again in 1996. John Kufuor became president in 2001, the first transfer of power between elected leaders in the fourth republic. Ghana produces cocoa and gold.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The 1992 constitution divides power between a president, parliament, cabinet, council of state and judiciary. Power has passed between the National Democratic Congress and the New Patriotic Party. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> Akufo-Addo was president from 2017 and was re-elected after the 2020 election. John Mahama won the 2024 election and returned as president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ghana.jpg" alt="Ghana" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Theodosia Okoh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ghana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1957 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Accra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kwame Nkrumah, Jerry John Rawlings, John Kufuor, Nana Akufo-Addo, John Mahama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional faiths</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Ghana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=ghana" aria-label="Ghana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4675 1970 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ghana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4995 2226L4995 2226M4995 2226L4955 2183M4995 2226L4972 2079M4995 2226L5013 2186"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4995 2226h0M4955 2183h0M4972 2079h0M5013 2186h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4995 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4995 2226h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Accra. <a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=ghana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
